It's true that many ascensions take much more than 35k turns, just
yesterday I ascended with "72912 pieces of gold, after 73614 moves" on
my current patch. But rather than adjusting the formula, I think I
just need to explain my reasoning a bit better. Consider this: the
highest level randomly generated monster is an arch-lich, level 29;
and a typical end-game character is around level 20. With the original
formula, the monster difficulty effectively maxes out at dungeon level
40; any deeper than that, and the return trip with the amulet, will
not be any harder. Now, when I said that a typical ascension was 35k
turns, what I really had in mind was that 35k turns is more than
enough time to be able to ascend. I just figure that 35k turns is
easily long enough for the character to be strong enough to ascend. At
35k turns, with my monster level formula, an arch-lich could spawn on
dungeon level 30 (rather than 40). I think that if a character has
taken 35k turns to reach dungeon level 30, then they should either be
strong enough to face the arch-lich, or they deserve defeat.

It's worth pointing out that what we're talking about here is the
_maximum_ level of randomly generated monsters. I haven't changed the
minimum level, which is dlvl/7. I've decided not to raise that for now
because although raising it may increase the difficulty a little, it
would allow for less variety of monsters.

I sometimes think it might be cool to have another few monsters
tougher than an arch-lich, which might show up deep in the dungeon in
long games, but that's not something I'm looking into at the moment.

