As I mentioned, I ascended a character yesterday with my latest patch.
(It was a trans-gender & alignment orcish barbarian.) I was pleased
enough with how the scaling of monster levels worked, but the mc nerf
didn't have as big of an effect as I expected. The only things I
noticed were that the level drain from wraiths & vampires was more
common =96 common enough to not want to be surrounded by them =96 and that
ghouls have a paralyzing attack that I didn't know about. I think the
MC nerf was an improvement, but a very minor one. I'm sure something
better could be done to fix the MC system. My barbarian still felt
pretty damn invincible.

I've got another few ideas to address the invincibility problem. I
haven't implemented any of these yet. I'm just posting them here for
feedback. You guys seem to be interested in this kind of stuff and I
really appreciate hearing other opinions.

First up: max hp. I'm not sure if everyone does this, but when I play
I stockpile potions to do mass alchemy for blessed full healing, then
I quaff them all at full hp to increase max hp. I almost never use
healing potions for any other purpose. With this strategy, I usually
end up with at least 400 max hp =96 which is far more than leveling up
can give. (My recent barbarian finished with 539 hp.) I reckon this is
too much hp. I think the full healing alchemy strategy is too
powerful, and I can think of a bunch of different ways it could be
addressed. I'll just list a few ways and you can tell me what you
think.

1) Remove stack-based alchemy. ie. to change alchemy so that dipping a
stack into a single potion will only mix one potion from the stack
rather than the entire stack. This would make it impractical to build
up many full-healing potions, because gain level and gain energy are
rare. It would also be more realistic. The potions would have to mix
in equal quantities, rather than 1 drop of gain level being enough to
upgrade 100 extra healing potions. Blessing stacks with holy water
could be an exception to the rule.

2) Put a limit on max hp; a fair value might be something like
maxhpmax =3D  (clvl+1) * 15. This would serve as a nice incentive to
level up, as well as being a big reduction in end game hp.

3) Reduce the max hp bonus from potions. +8 max hp is quite a lot, it
could be reduced outright, but I think the best way would be to halve
it for diluted potions. Since most of the full healing potions come
from alchemy, they are diluted. I think it makes sense that diluted
potions be less effective than undiluted potions. If blessed diluted
full healing potions only gave +4 max hp, that would cut my 540 hp
down to about 350, which is around about the same as the maxhpmax
number in #2.

So those are my idea for that 'problem'. #1 is probably good for
realism, but I think it's kind of fun to do alchemy en-mass, and I
think it adds a small level of inventory management strategy. So I'd
lean towards #2 or #3. I like #3 because it doesn't have hard limits
or new arbitrary numbers (except 1/2); but I like that #2 give more
incentive to level up. What do you think?

